id: rust.result
kind: rust
title: Result<T, E>
aliases: result, error handling, fallible
tags: errors, fundamentals
questions:
  - how do you handle errors in Rust?
invariant: A Result makes failure a value in the type, so the compiler forces every caller to decide what to do with it.
answer_30s: Result<T, E> encodes failure in the type instead of a global or an exception, so callers must handle it or explicitly propagate it with `?`.
answer_2m: |
  There is no hidden control flow: `match` consumes the value, `?` propagates
  it. Because errors are values they can be logged, retried, matched or wrapped,
  and the error type becomes part of the API: a library should expose a precise
  enum (callers can match), an application binary can flatten to anyhow at the
  boundary. Avoid unwrap in server code exactly where an outage would turn a bug
  into a crash loop restart.
when_to_use:
  - any operation that can fail: IO, parsing, network, validation
avoid_when:
  - genuinely impossible states (assert with a reason instead)
failure_modes:
  - unwrap on user input, turning a bad request into a panic and a 500
  - discarding the error with `let _ =`, losing the reason
misuse:
  - boxed dyn error everywhere => callers lose the ability to match and react
related:
  - rust.question-mark
  - rust.error-enum
  - rust.anyhow-thiserror
sources:
  - https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/result/enum.Result.html
  - https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch09-00-error-handling.html
